  "answer_text": "You **cannot legally buy or own a live Japanese dwarf flying squirrel** (*Pteromys momonga*) or its famously round, fluffy relative, the Ezo Momonga (*Pteromys volans orii*), as a pet. \n\nDespite their massive popularity on social media, they are not available in the global pet trade. \n\n### Why You Can\u2019t Buy One\n1. **Strict Protection Laws:** The Japanese dwarf flying squirrel is native only to the islands of Honshu, Shikoku, and Kyushu in Japan (while the Ezo Momonga is native to Hokkaido). Japan has incredibly strict conservation and environmental laws that prohibit the capture, domestic sale, or export of native wildlife.\n2. **No Captive Breeding:** There are no legal, established captive-breeding programs for *Pteromys momonga* anywhere in the world. Any live specimen found for sale outside of Japan would be the result of highly illegal, highly dangerous wildlife trafficking.\n3. **The Internet Misconception:** The viral videos and photos of these incredibly cute, round creatures are almost exclusively captured by wildlife photographers tracking them in their natural sub-alpine and evergreen forest habitats in Japan, not of individuals kept as household pets.\n\n---\n\n### Legal Alternatives You *Can* Buy\nIf you love the look and behavior of a tiny gliding mammal, there are highly similar, legal exotic pets that you can purchase from USDA-licensed breeders:\n\n#### 1. The Southern Flying Squirrel (*Glaucomys volans*)\n* **The Closest Match:** This species is native to North America and looks and behaves very similarly to the Japanese dwarf flying squirrel. \n* **Legality:** They are legal to own in many U.S. states, though some states require a permit or ban them entirely. \n* **Where to buy:** You can purchase hand-raised, captive-bred baby Southern Flying Squirrels from licensed, reputable exotic breeders in the United States (such as Janda Exotic Animal Ranch in Texas). They typically cost between **$300 and $450**.\n\n#### 2. Sugar Gliders (*Petaurus breviceps*)\n* **A Common Alternative:** While sugar gliders are marsupials (native to Australia and Indonesia) rather than rodents, they have the same adorable gliding membranes, big eyes, and pocket-sized appeal.\n* **Where to buy:** Sugar gliders are widely bred in captivity and can be bought from exotic pet stores, specialized breeders, or animal rescues worldwide. \n\n### A Note on Legality and Care\nBefore looking into any flying squirrel or sugar glider, **always check your state and local wildlife regulations**. Many states (such as California and Hawaii) and countries (such as the UK and parts of the EU) ban keeping flying squirrels or require strict wildlife-handling permits. \n\nAdditionally, flying squirrels are nocturnal, require exceptionally large and tall cages to glide, have specialized diets, and have highly sensitive emotional needs that require them to be kept in pairs to prevent depression.",
